\subsubsection{Sensitivity and degradation-case study}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}

The confirmatory comparison above is complemented by a prespecified
OU--III robustness study using the same ten paired wave-phase, IMU-noise, and
initialization seed triplets.  At the nominal $H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$ sea, one fixed
parameter at a time is multiplied by $0.5$, $0.75$, $1.0$, $1.25$, or $1.5$:
the OU correlation time $\tau$, stationary acceleration scale $\sigma_{aw}$,
and pseudo-measurement standard deviation $r_S$.  The remaining two parameters
stay at the single noise-free nominal operating point.  Thus this is a local
one-factor-at-a-time sensitivity analysis, not a global interaction study.
Because $\mat{R}_S=\operatorname{diag}(r_S^2)$, multiplying $r_S$ by $c$
multiplies the corresponding covariance by $c^2$.

Two deliberately difficult inputs delimit the demonstrated operating envelope.
The first scales the smallest JONSWAP record from $H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$ to
$H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$ without changing its spectral shape, placing wave motion
closer to the fixed IMU-noise floor.  The second compresses the controlled
$H_s=\SI{1.5}{m}$, $T_p=\SI{5.7}{s}$ to $H_s=\SI{4.0}{m}$,
$T_p=\SI{11.4}{s}$ ramp from \SI{360}{s} to \SI{30}{s}, while keeping the
transition midpoint, endpoint, long scoring window, and paired realizations
fixed.  These are degradation probes rather than additional population claims
about arbitrary ocean spectra.

