The three nominal sweep columns reproduce a mean normalized vertical error of
\OURobustnessNominalMean\% of $H_s$.  Across the declared multiplier range,
the largest mean is \OURobustnessWorstMean\% for
\texttt{\OURobustnessWorstParameter} at
$\times\OURobustnessWorstScale$; its paired difference from the nominal point
is \OURobustnessWorstDifference{} percentage points (bootstrap 95\% interval
$[\OURobustnessWorstDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessWorstDifferenceHigh]$).
Table~\ref{tab:ou_robustness_sensitivity} and
Fig.~\ref{fig:ou_robustness_sensitivity} report the complete sweep, so
robustness is assessed from the shape and intervals rather than from the most
favorable setting.

The sweep also exposes competing objectives.  Halving $r_S$ changes the 3D
displacement RMS by \OURobustnessRSHalfThreeDDifference{} m
($[\OURobustnessRSHalfThreeDLow,\OURobustnessRSHalfThreeDHigh]$) even though
it lowers the vertical-only score.  Halving $\sigma_{aw}$ changes pitch RMS by
\OURobustnessSigmaHalfPitchDifference{} degrees
($[\OURobustnessSigmaHalfPitchLow,\OURobustnessSigmaHalfPitchHigh]$).
Consequently, no sweep point is adopted as a replacement tuning: choosing one
from reference errors would violate the frozen, sensor-only tuning protocol and
would optimize one metric at the expense of another.

At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the mean absolute vertical RMS error is
\SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, corresponding to
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$, compared with
\OURobustnessLowReferenceMean\% at $H_s=\SI{0.27}{m}$.  The paired normalized
difference is \OURobustnessLowDifference{} points
($[\OURobustnessLowDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessLowDifferenceHigh]$), explicitly
showing the limit imposed by a nearly fixed absolute sensor-error floor.

Compressing the transition changes the Adaptive mean from
\OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of final $H_s$; the rapid-minus-controlled
paired difference is \OURobustnessRapidDifference{} points
($[\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceLow,\OURobustnessRapidDifferenceHigh]$).
Within the rapid case, Adaptive-minus-FixedNominal is
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationDifference{} points
($[\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationLow,\OURobustnessRapidAdaptationHigh]$).
Positive differences denote higher Adaptive error.  These results quantify,
rather than conceal, the response limit when the environmental time scale
changes much faster than the configured adaptation smoothing.
